{
 "action": "tool",
 "tool": "retrieve_history",
 "args": {
  "research_concepts": [
   "Defensive impact",
   "Half-to-half performance change (H2-H1)",
   "Team trait reliability",
   "Defensive suppression"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
   "Measurement of half-to-half performance differentials",
   "Attribution of performance changes to defensive strategy or execution",
   "Statistical methods for assessing trait reliability and consistency over time/games",
   "Confounding factors affecting half-to-half performance (e.g., score effects, fatigue, opponent adjustments)"
  ],
  "query": "Defense's effect on opponent H2-H1 change as a reliable team trait. Half-time adjustments, defensive consistency, team-level reliability."
 }
}

TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
 "research_concepts": [
  "Defensive impact",
  "Half-to-half performance change (H2-H1)",
  "Team trait reliability",
  "Defensive suppression"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "Measurement of half-to-half performance differentials",
  "Attribution of performance changes to defensive strategy or execution",
  "Statistical methods for assessing trait reliability and consistency over time/games",
  "Confounding factors affecting half-to-half performance (e.g., score effects, fatigue, opponent adjustments)"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [],
 "failed_approaches": [],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "No historical evidence was available for this question, so nothing is established."
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "No historical evidence was available, so all aspects of the research question remain open."
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "The concept of a 'defense's effect on opponents' H2-H1 change' requires careful operationalization, as many factors beyond defense can influence half-to-half performance shifts. Isolating the defensive component will be crucial.",
  "Assessing 'reliability' implies measuring this effect for each team across multiple games or seasons and then evaluating its consistency using statistical methods like intra-class correlation (ICC)."
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "The specific metric for 'H2-H1 change' (e.g., points, efficiency, shooting percentage) is not defined, leading to ambiguity in measurement.",
  "The precise mechanism or definition of 'defense's effect' is not specified, making attribution challenging.",
  "The expected magnitude of such a 'trait' and its inherent variance are unknown, which impacts power calculations."
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "No historical evidence was retrieved, so the decision was not influenced by prior project findings. This indicates the question is novel within the project's recorded history."
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": 0.7,
  "blocking_constraints": []
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "no",
  "cited": [],
  "summary": "No prior work on this specific research question was retrieved from historical records."
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "CONFOUNDING",
   "text": "Opponent H2-H1 performance change can be influenced by numerous factors unrelated to the specific defense, such as offensive adjustments, score effects (e.g., leading teams playing differently), player fatigue, coaching strategy, substitutions, and random variance. Isolating the 'defense's effect' is a significant challenge."
  },
  {
   "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
   "text": "The 'defense's effect on opponents' H2-H1 change' is a complex construct that needs to be precisely defined and measured. The reliability of this measurement itself within a single game or across a small set of games could be low due to noise and confounding factors, making it difficult to assess the reliability of the trait at the team level."
  },
  {
   "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
   "text": "While 120 team-seasons provide a reasonable number of units, if the true defensive trait effect is small or highly variable, detecting its reliability (e.g., a meaningful ICC) might require more data or a more precise measurement. Each team-season provides only one observation of the trait, which is then assessed for consistency across games within that season or across seasons."
  },
  {
   "code": "OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL",
   "text": "The 'H2-H1 change' is a derived metric, and interpreting it specifically as a 'defense's effect' requires strong assumptions and careful control for alternative explanations. There's a risk of misattributing changes that are primarily due to offensive adjustments or game state."
  },
  {
   "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
   "text": "Teams that have unusually good or bad first halves might naturally regress towards the mean in the second half, regardless of the defense played. This regression to the mean could be misinterpreted as a defensive effect or a lack of defensive effect."
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": 0.2,
  "rejected": 0.3,
  "untestable": 0.1,
  "artifact": 0.2,
  "uncertain": 0.2
 },
 "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "The intra-class correlation (ICC) of a team's estimated defensive impact on opponent's half-to-half (H2-H1) offensive efficiency change, across games within a regular season. The defensive impact will be estimated as the residual H2-H1 change in opponent offensive rating (ORtg) after controlling for known confounders.",
  "unit": "The primary unit for analysis is the game, nested within team-seasons. The reliability estimand will be calculated at the team-season level.",
  "sample": "All NBA regular season games from the 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, and 2025-26 seasons (up to 2026-04-13). Playoffs will be excluded due to differing strategic and motivational dynamics. This provides approximately 4 * 30 = 120 team-seasons for the reliability assessment, with ~82 games per team-season.",
  "power": "A pilot study or literature review will be conducted to estimate the expected variance components for defensive impact on H2-H1 change. A minimum detectable effect size for ICC (e.g., ICC > 0.1) will be determined, and the power of the available sample (120 team-seasons, ~82 games each) to detect this effect will be assessed. If power is insufficient, the scope of the question may be narrowed or the decision reconsidered.",
  "controls": "A mixed-effects regression model will be used to estimate the defensive impact on opponent H2-H1 ORtg change. Fixed effects will include: halftime score differential, opponent's season-to-date offensive rating (as a measure of inherent offensive strength), home/away status, back-to-back game status for the defensive team, and opponent's pre-game rest days. Random effects will capture team-level variation in defensive impact across games. The H2-H1 change in ORtg will be calculated as (ORtg_H2 - ORtg_H1) for the opponent.",
  "falsification": "The primary null hypothesis is that the ICC of the defensive impact on opponent H2-H1 ORtg change is zero, indicating no reliable team trait. Falsification would involve rejecting this null hypothesis with a statistically significant ICC value (e.g., p < 0.05).",
  "multiplicity": "Only one primary estimand (ICC for defensive impact on H2-H1 ORtg change) will be tested. No adjustments for multiple testing are planned unless secondary analyses (e.g., different H2-H1 metrics) are conducted, in which case appropriate adjustments (e.g., Bonferroni, FDR) will be applied.",
  "decision_rule": "The research question will be considered 'supported' if the estimated ICC for the defensive impact on opponent H2-H1 ORtg change is statistically significant (p < 0.05) and of a magnitude considered practically meaningful (e.g., ICC > 0.1). It will be 'rejected' if the ICC is not statistically significant and the study is adequately powered to detect a meaningful effect. It will be 'uncertain' if results are inconclusive due to insufficient power or high variance.",
  "data_version": "The analysis will use NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, and 2025-26 (partial) regular season and playoffs data as available on 2026-10-06, specifically relying on play-by-play, box-score, and derived play-type possession records from the NBA stats API."
 }
}

